      "text": "1. Start by using the equation $\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{w}$.\n2. Since $\\mathbf{w}$ is a vector, we can consider each coordinate term separately. Let $\\mathbf{u} = (u_x, u_y, u_z)$, $\\mathbf{v} = (v_x, v_y, v_z)$, and $\\mathbf{w} = (w_x, w_y, w_z)$. Then we have:\n   \\[\n   u_x v_y - u_y v_x + u_x = w_x, \\quad u_x v_z - u_z v_x + u_x = w_y, \\quad u_y v_z - u_z v_y + u_y = w_z\n   \\]\n3. Simplify each equation to find the individual terms. Recall that the cross product of $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ is a vector perpendicular to both vectors. Calculate the individual coordinate terms from $\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}$:\n   \\[\n   (u_x v_y - u_y v_x, u_x v_z - u_z v_x, u_y v_z - u_z v_y)\n   \\]\n4. Use the equation $\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{v}$:\n\\[\nw_x v - w_y u - w_z u = v_x, \\quad w_y u - w_z u = v_y, \\quad w_z u = v_z\n\\]\n5. Consider the vector triple product identity:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{b} (\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{c}) - \\mathbf{c} (\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b})\n\\]\n6. Apply this idea to compute $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w})$. Use each individual term of $\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$. We have:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w}) = (u_x v_y - u_y v_x) \\cdot (w_z, -w_z, 0) = w_x (u_yv_x - u_zv_y) - w_y (u_x v_y - u_y v_x) = w_x u_y v_x - w_x u_z v_y + w_y u_x v_y - w_y u_y v_x = w_x u_x v_y - w_x u_x v_y + w_y u_y v_x - w_y u_y v_x = \\mathbf{w} \\cdot (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v})\n   \\]\n7. We can derive from the given equations that the resulting inner product must be zero.\n8. Result conclusion:\n   \\boxed{1}",
