      "text": "Let $\\mathbf{p} = \\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w}.$  Then\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{p} &= (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) + \\mathbf{u} \\\\\n&= (-\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u}) + \\mathbf{u} \\\\\n&= \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{u} \\cdot (-\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u}) \\\\\n&= \\mathbf{u} - \\mathbf{u} \\cdot  (\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u}).\n\\end{align*}Therefore, $2 \\mathbf{p} = (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) + (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}),$ or $2 \\mathbf{p} = 2 \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}.$  Hence, $\\mathbf{p} = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nThen $\\begin{cases}\n\\mathbf{p} = \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{u} \\cdot (-\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u}) & \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 1, \\\\\n\\mathbf{p} = \\mathbf{u} - \\mathbf{u} \\cdot  (\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u}) & \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 1,\n\\end{cases}$ so we must have $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (-\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{u} \\cdot  (\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u}).$  Since the dot product of two vectors is commutative, we get\n\\[\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{w}.\\]Since $\\mathbf{w} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ we have $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 1,$ so $\\mathbf{u} = \\frac{\\mathbf{u}}{\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|} = \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\frac{\\mathbf{u}}{\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|} = \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 1.$  Similarly, $\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w} = \\frac{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w}}{\\|\\mathbf{w}\\|^{3/2}} = \\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w} = \\sqrt{1 + \\|\\mathbf{w}\\|^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 1 \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\|\\mathbf{w}\\|^{2}} = \\boxed{\\sqrt{2}}.$. The answer is \\sqrt{2}",
